<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valais</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valais is a canton in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> covering the upper valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, with Sion as its capital. Ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Sion from 999, it saw the communes of the German-speaking upper valley take power and conquer the French-speaking lower valley from Savoy in 1475. It joined the Swiss Confederation as a canton in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valais.jpg" alt="Valais" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian David · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Val%C3%A8re_et_Haut_de_Cry_-_juillet_2022.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…993 to 1032, tried to strengthen his position by granting counties to loyal bishops, giving the county of <b>Valais</b> to the bishop of Sion in 999 and the county of Vaud to the bishop of Lausanne in 1011, and in agreements from 1006 onwards he recognised his nephew,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Allied 'associates' such as the Abbey and City of St Gallen, the Three Leagues, the <b>Valais</b>, Geneva, Mulhouse and Neuchâtel stood around this core.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The so-called Long Diet, sitting in Zurich from 1814 to 1815, negotiated a new Federal Treaty, sworn on 7 August 1815, which bound twenty-two cantons together, including the newly admitted <b>Valais</b>, Neuchâtel and Geneva.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 999 King Rudolf III of Burgundy granted the bishop the county of <b>Valais</b>, making him a temporal lord.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-sion">Prince-Bishopric of Sion</a></li><li><blockquote>After decades of struggle, the Zenden forced Bishop Hildebrand Jost to abandon most of his temporal rights in 1628 and finally in 1634, and the <b>Valais</b> became a republic of seven tenths: Goms, Brig, Visp, Raron, Leuk, Sierre and Sion.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-the-seven-tenths">Republic of the Seven Tenths</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1802 he separated the <b>Valais</b> from the Helvetic Republic and made it a nominally independent Republic of the Rhône (République rhodanienne), under the joint protection of France, the Italian Republic and the Helvetic Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/rhodanic-republic">Rhodanic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
